1
00:00:00,706 --> 00:00:05,679
TicketPilot classifies support issues, recommends the right team,
and finds

2
00:00:05,719 --> 00:00:08,258
relevant fixes from resolved tickets.

3
00:00:09,081 --> 00:00:13,481
Follow one request, from a blocked employee to a confirmed
outcome.

4
00:00:15,416 --> 00:00:20,941
Meet Maya, a sales employee who cannot open her dashboard before a
customer meeting.

5
00:00:21,749 --> 00:00:26,913
In Northstar's employee portal, she describes the problem and
submits a request.

6
00:00:27,721 --> 00:00:30,871
She does not need to know which support team owns it.

7
00:00:31,693 --> 00:00:36,274
Her request now has a reference: I N C, twenty forty-eight.

8
00:00:39,556 --> 00:00:44,960
TicketPilot classifies Maya's issue and recommends Applications,
using similar

9
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:46,289
resolved tickets.

10
00:00:47,111 --> 00:00:52,196
Daniel, the support engineer, receives that classification with
matching cases

11
00:00:52,236 --> 00:00:53,928
and a suggested fix.

12
00:00:54,736 --> 00:00:59,664
He reviews the evidence before approving the route, and can
override it when needed.

13
00:01:07,396 --> 00:01:09,615
Daniel opens a matching case.

14
00:01:10,423 --> 00:01:15,268
Here, the request first went to Security, but Applications
resolved it.

15
00:01:16,090 --> 00:01:19,337
The notes describe restoring the reporting role mapping.

16
00:01:20,159 --> 00:01:22,823
That is the evidence behind the suggestion.

17
00:01:23,646 --> 00:01:28,615
Daniel can inspect the source before deciding whether it applies
to Maya's issue.

18
00:01:31,896 --> 00:01:34,462
TicketPilot has classified the issue.

19
00:01:35,284 --> 00:01:38,768
Daniel approves its proposed route to Applications.

20
00:01:39,576 --> 00:01:41,934
He can override that route if needed.

21
00:01:42,743 --> 00:01:45,143
The classification is already done.

22
00:01:48,196 --> 00:01:53,058
The suggested fix brings the relevant steps and a knowledge
reference into the same

23
00:01:53,098 --> 00:01:54,096
workspace.

24
00:01:54,918 --> 00:02:00,346
Daniel reviews the instructions, records the work, and sends Maya
an update.

25
00:02:01,154 --> 00:02:04,873
The access change is simulated in this portfolio demo.

26
00:02:05,696 --> 00:02:10,679
In a real deployment, an authorized team would perform and verify
that work.

27
00:02:13,946 --> 00:02:16,429
Maya returns to the same request.

28
00:02:17,237 --> 00:02:20,526
She can see what support did and what to check next.

29
00:02:21,334 --> 00:02:26,748
She opens her dashboard, verifies access, and selects Confirm it
works.

30
00:02:27,571 --> 00:02:31,123
If the issue remains, she can select Still need help.

31
00:02:31,932 --> 00:02:36,846
Closing a ticket is only useful when the employee's problem is
actually solved.

32
00:02:40,126 --> 00:02:42,998
Meet Elena, the IT service manager.

33
00:02:43,806 --> 00:02:49,030
She sees Maya's closed request alongside service health and how
engineers handled

34
00:02:49,070 --> 00:02:51,151
TicketPilot's recommendations.

35
00:02:51,973 --> 00:02:56,692
The goal is fewer avoidable transfers and less repeated
investigation.

36
00:02:57,514 --> 00:03:00,151
These dashboard numbers are sample data.

37
00:03:00,973 --> 00:03:06,623
A pilot must measure actual handoffs, resolution time, and
employee outcomes.

38
00:03:11,016 --> 00:03:13,888
For employees, one request to track.

39
00:03:14,696 --> 00:03:19,596
For support, an owner recommendation and a relevant fix with
sources.

40
00:03:20,404 --> 00:03:23,763
For leaders, visibility into service outcomes.

41
00:03:24,571 --> 00:03:28,041
That is the value proposition to validate in a pilot.

42
00:03:31,316 --> 00:03:33,618
Explore the guided demo yourself.

43
00:03:34,427 --> 00:03:40,035
Then read the product case study for the evidence, product
decisions, and rollout plan.
